.TH CSTRING_DESTROY 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cstring_destroy \- destroy a string and set its pointer to NULL
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cstring.h>

cstring_destroy(s);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Destroys the cstring. It frees the internal buffer and the container
itself. It then sets the pointer variable s to NULL. A second call is
therefore safe.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
s   cstr lvalue to destroy, such as a variable or an element of an
    array. The macro evaluates it exactly once, and sets it to NULL
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Safe to call with a NULL pointer (a no-op)
The function frees the internal buffer and the container with the
allocator that created the string. That allocator is the custom
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t that the caller gave to cstring_create_full(). It
is free() when the caller gave none
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cstr s = cstring_create("hello", NULL);
cstring_destroy(s);   /* s is now NULL */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cstr_destroy (3)
